meta {
  name: 03 - Submit Document (Sync Direct Response)
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/ocr/process
  body: multipartForm
  auth: none
}

headers {
  X-API-Key: {{apiKey}}
}

body:multipart-form {
  file: @file(../test_data_june/StockSales_GENXPHARMADERMAGLOW_62026_260704_172654.pdf) @contentType(application/pdf)
  ~project_name: VendorBillsApp
  ~user_id: fin_user_01
  ~document_type: AUTO
  ~async_mode: false
  ~response_format: full
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.body && res.body.data && res.body.data.id) {
    bru.setEnvVar("docId", res.body.data.id);
  }
}

docs {
  Same endpoint as 01, but blocks until processing finishes and returns the
  full result inline. This is what you get when async_mode is false - and false
  is the SERVER DEFAULT, so omitting async_mode entirely lands here.

  ## Response

  success, and a data object - NOT request_id/status like the async form. The
  data object carries id, filename, document_type, language, has_handwriting,
  pages, markdown, structured_data, processing_time, status, created_at, and
  request_id.

  This response includes structured_data. So does the asynchronous one - an
  earlier version of this collection said otherwise (see 02).

  There is no `tables` block and no plain_text - both repeated content already
  in the payload. See the collection README.

  ## response_format

  `full` (the default) is the shape above. Set it to `erp` and `data` becomes
  the business projection instead - named field values and object-keyed line
  items, no markdown or verification evidence. See request 11 for the shape.
  Anything else returns 400.

  ## Caution

  A large or multi-page document can hold the connection open for minutes.
  Prefer 01 + 02 for anything non-trivial, and set a generous client timeout
  if you use this form.

  ## Errors

  - 400 no valid file uploaded
  - 500 processing failed
}
